WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:09.216
BEN: Hi, Little Fox readers, and welcome to a new edition of People in the News .

2
00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:15.706
This week we'll be talking about Muhammad Yunus. He's a banker and economist who has dedicated his life

3
00:00:15.707 --> 00:00:23.866
to helping people escape poverty—isn't that right, Olivia? OLIVIA: It sure is, Ben. And when you learn

4
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:30.031
more about Muhammad, it's easy to see why he became so passionate about this cause. He was

5
00:00:30.032 --> 00:00:35.028
born in 1940 in Bangladesh, one of the poorest countries in the world. As a young boy, Muhammad

6
00:00:35.029 --> 00:00:41.757
was influenced by his mother's generosity. Even though the family wasn't wealthy, she always gave what she

7
00:00:41.758 --> 00:00:50.004
could to beggars who came to the door. Neither she nor Muhammad's father was able to finish

8
00:00:50.005 --> 00:00:55.678
school, but they both wanted their kids to get a good education. There were ten children in

9
00:00:55.679 --> 00:01:06.172
the family, and every one of them finished college. Muhammad was a very hard-working student. After college he

10
00:01:06.173 --> 00:01:12.155
was offered a Fulbright Scholarship, which allowed him to travel to the U.S. to study. He earned

11
00:01:12.156 --> 00:01:19.646
a PhD in economics and then worked in the U.S. as a college professor. In 1972 he

12
00:01:19.647 --> 00:01:24.660
accepted a position teaching economics at Chittagong University in Bangladesh. Back home, Muhammad was overwhelmed by the conditions

13
00:01:24.661 --> 00:01:32.969
surrounding him. The country's coast had been severely damaged by a cyclone, and tens of thousands of

14
00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:39.301
people had died from a horrible famine. Now the streets were crowded with people who were so

15
00:01:39.302 --> 00:01:49.138
starved, they looked like skeletons. Muhammad began to realize that the abstract economic theories he taught to students

16
00:01:49.139 --> 00:01:54.731
every day were not going to do anything to help the starving people around him. So one

17
00:01:54.732 --> 00:01:58.864
day Muhammad took his students on a field trip to a rural village. BEN: Hmm . .

18
00:01:58.865 --> 00:02:08.183
. interesting. What did they do there? OLIVIA: They talked to poor people, to learn more about

19
00:02:08.184 --> 00:02:15.270
their lives. That day Muhammad met a woman who made bamboo stools for a living. The woman explained

20
00:02:15.271 --> 00:02:21.521
that she had no money to buy the bamboo, so she had to borrow money from a

21
00:02:21.522 --> 00:02:29.928
bamboo dealer. After the woman sold her stools, most of her earnings went right back to the

22
00:02:29.929 --> 00:02:39.088
bamboo dealer. Since he charged her lots of interest, she was left with almost no profit—only two cents

23
00:02:39.089 --> 00:02:46.508
for every stool she sold. The woman also told Muhammad something very important: if she had just

24
00:02:46.509 --> 00:02:52.070
twenty cents, she wouldn't have to borrow money from the bamboo dealer. Then she'd be able to

25
00:02:52.071 --> 00:03:00.562
increase her profits and have a much better life. The woman's story affected Muhammad deeply. He was amazed

26
00:03:00.563 --> 00:03:08.397
that all she needed to rise out of poverty was twenty cents. Muhammad talked to other women

27
00:03:08.398 --> 00:03:15.557
in the village, and the women all had similar stories. So he decided to try an experiment.

28
00:03:15.558 --> 00:03:22.546
He lent money to 42 women, spending just $27, less than one dollar per person. He set very

29
00:03:22.547 --> 00:03:29.038
fair terms for these loans and simply trusted the borrowers to pay back the money when they

30
00:03:29.039 --> 00:03:36.457
could. By giving the businesswomen tiny or "micro" loans, he was able to help them earn profits.

31
00:03:36.458 --> 00:03:42.789
Aware that he didn't have enough money to keep making loans himself, Muhammad went to a bank

32
00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:48.217
for help. But the banker there didn't trust poor people to pay the money back; he said he

33
00:03:48.218 --> 00:03:53.690
could only lend money to people who had money. Muhammad thought this was really unfair—poor people needed

34
00:03:53.691 --> 00:04:00.268
money more than anyone else! Soon he began borrowing money himself and then lending it to poor

35
00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:08.928
people. Despite what the banker had thought, the borrowers always paid the money back. Before long Muhammad was

36
00:04:08.929 --> 00:04:16.357
lending money to people in about one hundred villages throughout Bangladesh. Muhammad's system, now known as microlending,

37
00:04:16.358 --> 00:04:24.429
was working so well that he started Grameen Bank in 1983. The bank is mostly owned by

38
00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.990
the borrowers themselves, and it only lends money to people who are extremely poor. Amazingly, the bank has

39
00:04:28.991 --> 00:04:34.495
lent money to nearly eight million people and performed better than most traditional banks. It has also

40
00:04:34.496 --> 00:04:41.820
inspired similar programs in many other countries. Grameen Bank also offers financial advice and education to its

41
00:04:41.821 --> 00:04:49.400
borrowers. All borrowers are urged to follow the "16 Decisions," a set of guidelines for positive social

42
00:04:49.401 --> 00:04:56.225
change. Borrowers promise to grow vegetables year round, send their children to school, and keep their homes in

43
00:04:56.226 --> 00:05:02.645
good repair. Borrowers are also expected to invest the money in their businesses since the loan is

44
00:05:02.646 --> 00:05:06.721
not charity. Through these efforts Grameen Bank has enabled many poor people to run successful businesses. Muhammad

45
00:05:06.722 --> 00:05:15.889
started other businesses that directly help poor people, including a health care company that provides medical services to

46
00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:24.707
poor villages and a phone company that brings mobile phones to remote rural areas. Because of this,

47
00:05:24.708 --> 00:05:31.460
many villages in Bangladesh now have a "telephone lady," who earns money by charging other villagers to

48
00:05:31.461 --> 00:05:37.698
use her cell phone. BEN: Telephone lady . . . That's an interesting job. It sounds like Muhammad's

49
00:05:37.699 --> 00:05:46.699
efforts have mainly helped women. OLIVIA: Yes, Ben, that's true. Ninety-seven percent of Grameen Bank's borrowers are

50
00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:52.166
female. That's because Muhammad noticed that female business owners nearly always use their earnings to feed their

51
00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:57.084
kids and take care of other household needs. He came to see that lending money to women

52
00:05:57.085 --> 00:06:00.158
impacted entire families. BEN: Just one more question, Olivia. Why did Muhammad win the Nobel Peace Prize? What

53
00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:04.917
does microlending have to do with peace? OLIVIA: Well, Muhammad believes that poverty breeds violence and war.

54
00:06:04.918 --> 00:06:12.067
He says that when people are earning enough money for food and other basic needs, society tends

55
00:06:12.068 --> 00:06:17.391
to be peaceful. By fighting poverty, he's helping to make the world a more peaceful place. BEN: That's

56
00:06:17.392 --> 00:06:29.139
awesome! Thanks, Olivia, for telling us about this incredible man. See you next week, Little Fox readers.

